				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2902864&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607633170" alt="MaliceAforethought" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2902864)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;" >MaliceAforethought</a></span> <span class="odate time_1524960408 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">29 Apr 2018 00:06</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3783984">
									

<p>Story time. There's a thing we have here in merry old England called the National Trust, which is like a charity that preserves historical buildings, sites, and the like. There are quite a few stately homes within a not-too-long drive of where I live, so we go fairly often for a day out.</p>
<p>Now, the thing about National Trust properties is that they often run events and stuff. Tours and Easter egg hunts and things. On one occasion, it was a man with a load of different creatures in terrariums and cages, which he showed off in front of an audience of kids. I was an edgy 14 year-old at this point, and so stood at the back rolling my eyes and trying to look unimpressed.</p>
<p>Cue five minutes later, and he's letting people hold bugs. Specifically a giant millipede curled up into a ball, which he let people pass around. A small child then turns around and, without warning, places it into my hand. It starts to uncurl, and I can feel its legs on my skin, and I nearly drop it and scream. I couldn't though, so I had to hold it for about a minute before someone finally took it off me. As someone who mildly dislikes all kinds of bugs in general, and things with lots of legs specifically, it was awful. I'm not saying this was the subconscious inspiration for the scip, but it was <em>totally the subconscious inspiration for the scip</em>.</p>
<p>TL;DR: Millipedes are freaky and I have the anecdotes to prove it.</p>

